Plastic is something we often use in our daily lives. As small as plastic bags, baby bottles, beverage bottles, lunch boxes, plastic wrap, as large as agricultural film, furniture, electrical appliances, 3D printing, and even rockets and missiles, plastics are all present.
1. According to the behavior when heated, plastics can be divided into thermoplastics and thermosetting sciences according to their behavior when heated;
According to the type of reaction during the synthesis of the resin in the plastic, the resin can be divided into polymerized plastics and polycondensed plastics;
According to the order state of the resin macromolecules, plastics can be divided into two types: amorphous plastics and crystalline plastics;
Polyethylene (PE) has excellent processing and use properties, is the most widely used variety in synthetic resins, and its production capacity has long ranked first among all plastic varieties. Polyethylene resins mainly include low-density polyethylene (LDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), and high-density polyethylene (HDPE).
Vietnam's "Vietnam+" reported on July 21, 2021. The Ministry of Industry and Trade of Vietnam revealed that the main reason for the recent slow development of the automotive auxiliary industry is that Vietnam's automotive market is relatively small, only one-third of Thailand's and one-fourth of Indonesia's.
Recently, in order to actively ensure the supply of spare parts and increase the domestic content, some large domestic enterprises in Vietnam have actively increased their investment in the automotive auxiliary industry. Among them, THACO AUTO has invested in the construction of Vietnam's largest spare parts production industrial park with 12 factories in Quang Nam Province to increase the localized content of automobiles and their spare parts.
In order to remove the above-mentioned obstacles, the Ministry of Industry and Trade of Vietnam proposes to plan and build an infrastructure system to meet the needs of people, especially the residents of major cities such as Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City.
At present, the purchasing power of packaged food in South Africa mainly comes from the middle and upper income class, while the low-income group mainly buys bread, dairy products and oil and other staple food. According to the data, 36% of the food expenditure of low-income households in South Africa is spent on cereals such as corn flour, bread and rice, while the higher income families spend only 17% of their food expenditure.
The change of people's lifestyle, the development of import economy, the formation of packaging recycling trend, the progress of technology and the transformation from plastic to glass packaging will be the important factors affecting the development of packaging industry in South Africa in the next few years.
